<circuit version="1.1.0-SR2" rev="261109" stepSize="1000000" stepsPS="20000" NLsteps="100000" reaStep="1000000" animate="0" >
<description>
Negative impedance converter (NIC): an op amp makes the input port (U1's + input, tunnel PORT) look like a resistor of -1 kOhm.

How it works:
- U1 has gain 2 (R1 = R2 = 10k), so its output is 2 * Vport.
- Z (1k) from the output back to the port drives a current (2*Vport - Vport)/Z = Vport/Z *into* the port: the port behaves like -Z.
- The source V1 (1 V) drives the port through Rs = Ra (500 Ohm), or Ra + Rb (2k) with S1 open.

S1 closed (Rs = 500 Ohm):
- Vport = 1 V * (-1k)/(500 - 1k) = 2 V, Vout = 4 V.
- The current in Ra is (1 V - 2 V)/500 = -2 mA: it flows back into the 1 V source. That is a negative resistance at work.

S1 open (Rs = 2k): the positive feedback (Rs/(Rs+Z) = 2/3) is stronger than the negative feedback (R2/(R1+R2) = 1/2). The circuit is unstable and latches at a rail (+/-12 V).

Scope: PORT, OUT. Speed 2 %.

What to try: open S1 and watch it latch; close it again. With these values the circuit is stable while Rs &lt; Z (1k): try Rb values around that limit.

Results [SIMULATED]: S1 closed: Vport 2.000 V, Vout 4.000 V, I(Ra) -2.000 mA. S1 open: latched at 12 V.

Why it is here: a test circuit for the op-amp model (BH-032). It has positive and negative feedback at the same time; the model must find the stable solution, and must latch when there is none.
</description>


